// Order_Assignment form workflow: On Load.
// Narrows the Employee lookup to active supervisors.
//
// Copy of filterOperatorLookup with the designation string changed - read that
// file's header for why this is done in Deluge rather than as a filter
// criterion on the field. Short version: Designation is a dropdown with no
// configured options, so the form builder has nothing to build a criterion from.
//
// WHY THIS FORM MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. Order_Assignment is where a plan
// gets its supervisor - createProductionPlans reads it per Order_Source and
// stamps the result on Production_Planning.Assigned_To. Every downstream screen
// filters on that. So a store person picked here does not just look wrong on
// this form; he ends up owning a production plan, appearing in the production
// tab's supervisor filter, and being mailed about material he cannot act on.
// This is the cheapest place to stop that.
//
// Not guarded with a fallback, unlike the picker in getSupervisorMaterials. That
// one is the login stand-in and an empty list there is a dead screen; this is an
// admin form used occasionally, and an empty picker is a visible prompt to go
// and set the designations rather than something that blocks the floor.

// BEFORE anything touches the field - `clear` and ui.add both take the
// selection with them on a lookup, and re-adding the choices does not bring it
// back. On a new record this is simply empty.
savedEmp = input.Employee;

// sort by on its own assignment, never in a for-each header - inline is what
// made the first version of the operator file refuse to save at all.
supList = Employee[Designation == "Supervisor" && Status == "Active"] sort by Employee_Name;

Employee:ui.add(supList.ID.getAll());

// Put the selection back. Assigning a value uses input., unlike the client task
// above which names the field bare.
input.Employee = savedEmp;
